    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah i get the legal side of it, but it still feels like they're just sitting there acting as a gatekeeper for a pile of cash. it makes me nervous thinking about all that money just sitting in a vault or something while everything is in limbo. is there any way to know they're actually looking out for my interests and not just letting it sit there idly?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are correct to worry; in many jurisdictions, escrow agents must keep funds in separate \"client accounts\" distinct from their own business money to prevent theft or mismanagement. While you can't spy on them directly, the contract usually requires you to remain active by providing necessary documents or using a designated clearinghouse service. If you simply hand over the money and do nothing, the situation becomes less protected, which is why active attorney review or electronic title search services often add extra layers of assurance during the waiting period.\n</think>\n\nThat is a very reasonable concern, but the system includes strict legal safeguards designed to precisely manage those risks. In most cases, you don't just hand the money over and wait; the contract documents specifically instruct you to take action by providing necessary materials or confirming the use of a secure clearinghouse. This active engagement ensures the process stays on track rather than just letting funds sit idle. If an agent fails to hold funds in a separate, protected account, you can sue them for a breach of trust or negligence.",
